% AUTHOR: implementation-absys (an agent: Claude Opus 5.5, claude-opus-5-5). One text for absys, absys-depth-first and absys-wake-back.
% PRECURSOR-SEEN: no. Not any E01-E09 program (see programs/absys/README.md, batch 1C, for what was read).
% NOTATION: 1969 (.ab1), the earliest notation with every construct used (systems/absys/README.md, Notation), as for loop (P04) and rev, conc (P03).
% IDIOM: a relation as name'=lambda x begin {A or B} end, primes for new local names, lists [a;b] and [h&t]: Foster and Elcock 1969 (map and item, examples/map.ab1, examples/triples.ab1);
% IDIOM: the shape of the two cases, {R = [], S = T} or {R = [X|L], T = [X|N], app(L, S, N)}: Elcock 1990 p. 8 (examples/append.abs);
% IDIOM: loop as a procedure whose body applies itself, loop = lambda X {loop(X)}: the reconstruction's tutorial, ch. 5.
% P09a. Unknowns x, b. One stage, two computations spawned (harnesses/absys/README.md, 3.6): A loop(x); B rev(<seq>, b).
% One text for both instances, P09a.AB and P09a.BA: the harness gives the SPAWN subsections in the instance's spawn_order.
% Absys has one computation per session; the harness accepts B's text into the computation that holds A (QUESTIONS, A5).
% CONSTANTS BEGIN
ra = [1;2;3]   % stage 1, computation B: rev(ra, b)
% CONSTANTS END
% loop, P04 Vocabulary, its one case: loop(x) if loop(x).
loop'=lambda p
    begin loop(p) end
% conc, P03 (P01) Vocabulary, cases 1 and 2 in that order; case 2 as its head equations, then conc(a, b, c).
conc'=lambda p, q, r
    begin {p = [] q = r or
           p = [h'&k'] r = [h&m'] conc(k, q, m)} end
% rev, P03 Vocabulary, cases 1 and 2 in that order; case 2 as its head equation, then rev(l, q), then conc(q, <h>, r).
rev'=lambda p, r
    begin {p = [] r = [] or
           p = [h'&k'] rev(k, n') conc(n, [h], r)} end
% STAGE 1
% SPAWN A
loop(x)
% SPAWN B
rev(ra, b)
